    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**Prototype pollution** is a security incident/scenario. Start with impact and attacker capability, isolate the broken control, contain exposure, fix the root cause, and add a regression guard.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the protected asset, attacker capability, trust boundary, secure default, enforcement point, and recovery path before writing code.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core policy or state check so you can explain the security reasoning line by line during the interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface IncidentCheck {\n  control: string;\n  failed: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction brokenControls(\n  checks: readonly IncidentCheck[],\n): string[] {\n  const result: string[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < checks.length; i += 1) {\n    if (checks[i].failed) {\n      result.push(checks[i].control);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst broken = brokenControls([\n  { control: \"authorization\", failed: true },\n  { control: \"csp\", failed: false },\n]);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or browser APIs when they make the same control clearer without hiding the security boundary.\n\n```ts\nconst checks = [\n  { control: \"authorization\", failed: true },\n  { control: \"csp\", failed: false },\n];\n\nconst broken = checks\n  .filter((check) => check.failed)\n  .map((check) => check.control);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nTreat Prototype pollution as a production incident: confirm impact, contain the blast radius, identify the failed trust boundary, remediate the root cause, and verify recovery.\n\nStep 6 — Validation:\nTest the intended path, denied path, malformed input, missing/expired state, misconfiguration, and recovery/rollback behavior where applicable.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what is trusted, what is untrusted, where final enforcement occurs, why the default is safe, and how monitoring or testing prevents recurrence.",
